WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:11.412
Some numbers are huge, like 47,318.
Some sit below zero, like minus 6.

2
00:00:11.632 --> 00:00:16.880
Where a digit stands decides its
worth. Today you will read both kinds.

3
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.235
Read this number: three
thousand, four hundred and six.

4
00:00:21.455 --> 00:00:25.402
Three thousands, four hundreds,
no tens, and six ones.

5
00:00:25.622 --> 00:00:30.591
The zero is not nothing to skip.
It holds the tens column open.

6
00:00:30.811 --> 00:00:37.313
Take the zero away and 3,406 turns
into 346, a much smaller number.

7
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:40.969
The same 8 is worth 8 in 58, but 80 in 85.

8
00:00:41.189 --> 00:00:43.511
And eighty thousand in 80,000.

9
00:00:44.011 --> 00:00:49.584
Here is 4,208: four thousands, two
hundreds, no tens and eight ones.

10
00:00:49.804 --> 00:00:54.680
Swap one thousand for ten hundreds.
That swap is called regrouping.

11
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.312
Now the same number is three thousands,
twelve hundreds and eight ones.

12
00:00:59.532 --> 00:01:05.291
It is still 4,208. Regrouping changes
how it is stored, never its worth.

13
00:01:05.791 --> 00:01:10.109
Times 10 does not add a zero.
Every digit moves one place left.

14
00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:14.788
Take 34 times 10. The 3
tens become 3 hundreds.

15
00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.469
The 4 ones become 4 tens, too.

16
00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:23.215
34 times 10 is 340. The zero
only fills the empty ones column.

17
00:01:23.435 --> 00:01:29.937
Times 100 moves every digit two
places. 34 times 100 is 3,400.

18
00:01:30.437 --> 00:01:34.663
Numbers keep going below zero. A
thermometer is where you meet them.

19
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:38.830
It is 3 degrees, and then
it gets 5 degrees colder.

20
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:43.927
Count down one step at a time:
2, 1, 0, minus 1, minus 2.

21
00:01:44.147 --> 00:01:48.791
The temperature is now minus 2
degrees, two steps below zero.

22
00:01:49.011 --> 00:01:52.958
Zero is a number you pass
through, not where counting stops.

23
00:01:53.458 --> 00:01:57.127
On a number line, further
right always means bigger.

24
00:01:57.347 --> 00:01:59.437
That keeps working below zero.

25
00:01:59.657 --> 00:02:02.489
Minus 2 sits to the right of minus 9.

26
00:02:02.709 --> 00:02:08.468
Minus 2 is bigger. Minus 9 is
nine below zero; minus 2 is two.

27
00:02:08.688 --> 00:02:12.635
Smallest first, these go minus
8, minus 3, zero, then 5.

28
00:02:13.135 --> 00:02:18.662
Rounding asks one question: which
neighbour is this number nearer to?

29
00:02:18.882 --> 00:02:25.290
Round 4,630 to the nearest hundred.
Its neighbours are 4,600 and 4,700.

30
00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:30.201
It is 30 past 4,600,
but 70 short of 4,700.

31
00:02:30.421 --> 00:02:34.879
So 4,630 rounds down to
4,600, the nearer one.

32
00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:40.904
Exactly halfway needs a rule:
4,650 rounds up, to 4,700.

33
00:02:41.404 --> 00:02:45.816
A digit's column decides its
worth. A zero holds a column open.

34
00:02:46.036 --> 00:02:50.122
Regroup a thousand into ten hundreds
and the number never changes.

35
00:02:50.342 --> 00:02:53.825
Times 10 moves every digit
one place to the left.

36
00:02:54.045 --> 00:02:59.154
Below zero, further right is bigger.
Rounding picks the nearer neighbour.
